<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>





<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 10 Practice</title>

    <p workspace=".2in">
        <em>Clearly and correctly disprove a statement using a counterexample.
        </em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How to search for appropriate counterexamples (being strategic, e.g., trying odd/even, or 0,1,or 2 mod 3,
                        or positive/negative).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                     How to explain a counterexample, for example, how your example satisfies the hypothesis but not the conclusion.
                    </p>
                </li>

            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice From Class</title>

        <p>
            Weekly Practice 6: # 1,  Weekly Practice 1: Activity 3.4, Countrexamples you wrote up in the 
            proof portfolio.
        </p>

    </paragraphs>


    
<exercise>
            <introduction>
               <p> Each of thte following statements is incorrect. Show the statement is false using a counterexample. 
                You should clearly explain why the counterexample you found shows the statement is false.
 </p>
            </introduction>
    
                    <task>
                        <statement>
                        
                        <p>
                            
                           If <m>n</m> is an integer, then <m>n^{2} \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.
                                                       (Note - this statement is equivalent to <q> For all integers <m>n</m>,
                                                       <m>n^{2} \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.</q>)
                        </p>
            
                        </statement>
                        <solution>
                            <p>
                             In this case, I would consider <m>n\in\{0,1,2\}</m> as examples 
                             since the problem is about an integer modulo 3, so there
                             are only 3 possible cases. While, <m>1^2\equiv 1\pmod{3}</m>
                             and <m>2^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>, <m>0^2 \not\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m>.
                             So, we've found a counterexample. In fact, there are infinitely
                             many counterexamples, any multiple of <m>3</m> will disprove
                             this statement.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                             On the quiz, I'd write the following. Note <m>0\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
                             and <m>0^2 \equiv 0\pmod{3}</m> as <m>3\mid 0^2-0</m>. 
                             So <m>0^2\not\equiv 1 \pmod{3}</m> and the statement is false.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For each integer <m>n</m>, <m>n^{2}+1</m> is a prime number.
                        </p>
                         </statement>
                         <solution>
                            <p>
                                Technically <m>1</m> is not a prime number,
                                so <m>n=0</m> would disprove this statement. 
                                Otherwise, the first positive counterexample
                                is <m>n=3</m> which gives <m>n^2 + 1 = 3^2 + 1 = 10</m>.
                                Since <m>10=5\cdot 2</m>, <m>10</m> is not prime.
                            </p>
                            <p>
                                On the quiz, I'd write the following. Let <m>n=10\in\mathbb{Z}</m>.
                                Then <m>n^2 + 1 = 3^2 + 1 = 10</m>.
                                Since <m>10=5\cdot 2</m>, <m>10</m> is not prime. This
                                shows the statement is false.
                            </p>
                         </solution>
                    </task>
                       

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For all integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, if <m>x</m> is even and <m>y</m> is odd,
                                                        then <m>2x-y</m> is even.
                        </p>
                        
                                                </statement>
                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                     Start by testing for an even integer <m>x</m> and 
                                                     an odd integer <m>y</m> since we want the hypothesis
                                                     to be true.
                                                    </p>
                                                    <p>
                                                     Let <m>x = 2</m> and <m>y=3</m>. Since <m>x=2</m> is an even integer and 
                                                     <m>y=3</m> is an odd integer these two integers satisfy our hypotheses. 
                                                     Then <m>2x-y=2(2)-3 = 1</m>. Since <m>y</m> is an odd integer,
                                                     the conclusion is false. Thus <m>x=2</m> and <m>y=3</m> is 
                                                     a counterexample and the statement is false.
                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                        If <m>x</m> is a real number then <m>x^{2}&gt;x</m>.
                        </p>
                                                </statement>

                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                      This is false. In this one, you might try
                                                      positive real numbers, negative real numbers, then 
                                                      <m>x=0</m>. The real number <m>x=0</m> gives a counterexample
                                                        since we get equality, <m>0^2 = 0</m>, so <m>0</m> is not
                                                        strictly greater than <m>x=0</m>.You could also choose any
                                                        positive
                                                        fraction (or real number) that is less than <m>1</m>.
                                                        In fact, any element
                                                        of the set <m>\{x\in\mathbb{R}: 0&lt;x&lt;1\}</m> is a 
                                                        counterexample.
                                                    </p>
                                                    <p>
                                                      Let <m>x=\frac{1}{2}\in\mathbb{R}</m>. Then <m>x^2 = \frac{1}{4}</m>
                                                      and <m>\frac{1}{4}&lt; \frac{1}{2}</m>. So, in the case of <m>x=\frac{1}{2}</m> 
                                                      <m>x^2&lt; x</m> and we have found a counterexample. Therefore
                                                      the statement is false.

                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>

                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For all real numbers <m>x, y,</m> and <m>z</m>, if <m>|x-y|&lt; 4</m> and <m>|y-z| &lt; 4</m>$ then $<m>|x-z|&lt;4</m>.
                        </p>
                                                </statement>
                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                      This is false. Choose <m>x=0</m>, <m>y=3</m> and <m>z=6</m>.
                                                      Note <m>x,y,z\in\mathbb{R}</m>. Moreover, <m>|x-y| = 3 &lt; 4</m>
                                                      and <m>|y-z|=3&lt; 4 </m>. So our hypotheses are 
                                                      satisfied. However, <m>|x-z| = 6 &gt; 4</m>, so the conclusion
                                                      is false in this case. Thus, <m>x=0</m>, <m>y=3</m> and <m>z=6</m> 
                                                      is a counterexample and the statement is false.
                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>

                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For all integers <m>n</m>, if <m>n^{2}-n</m> is even, then <m>n</m> is even.
                        </p>

        
                                                </statement>
                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                     Start with choosing an odd integer, since
                                                     we want the conclusion to be false. Suppose <m>n = 3</m>.
                                                     Then <m>n^2 - n = 3^2 - 3 = 6</m> which is even. However, <m>n=3</m>
                                                     is an odd integer. So <m>n=3</m> is a counterexample and the statement is false.
                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>
                    </task>

               
                                               

                   
                    </exercise>


        </handout>